  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nAM UPDATE: \n12Z guidance continues to hone in on scattered discrete cells firing\noff across the Tri State after 2 PM today then congealing into more \nlinear segment. While some isolated severe wind gusts are possible, \nforecast DCAPE and CAPE values are not robust. \n\nThere is a conditional tornadic threat later today, particularly for \nthe Tri-State, but much will hinge on how much clearing we have \nafter the AM rain showers. Large scale cyclonic motion/ascent over \nthe area, bulk shear around 35-40 knots, and 0-1 SRH values between \n100 to 150 m2/s2 near the Tri-State all suggest that isolated \ntornadic activity is possible. Similarly, machine learning such as \nNadocast and CSU echo this threat with 2-5% contours across \nsoutheastern Indiana and portions of the Tri-State. However, as \nmentioned previously, forecast instability appears more limited, \nwith only a wedge of 1000-1500 J/kg of SBCAPE moving into the Tri-\nState this evening. Much will depend on a dry slot pivoting through/ \nhow much cloud clearing we can advect in. \n\nOutside of the severe threat, we do still have concerns for locally \nheavy rainfall. While this morning's 12z balloon sampled PWATs just \nabove the 75th percentile, as the low pivots, additional moisture \nwill be advected in this afternoon and evening. For areas that have \nskinny CAPE present, high PWATs, and  the possibility for both slow \nmoving and training storms, localized flooding remains a concern. \n\n---\nPrevious discussion--> Upstream lightning activity has decreased \nsignificantly and will continue the trend as it moves into the CWA \nlater this morning. Showers that have crossed the CWA to the ene \nearly this morning have produced a trace of rainfall and are just \nprepping the atmosphere for a round of showers and thunderstorms this\nmorning. There will be breaks in the showers at times, and the \nthunderstorms will be associated with vorticity maxima that are not \ncurrently present to be timed with any certainty. Tornadic threat is \ndue to closely spaced low pressure centers at the surface and a \nlittle more to the west aloft. Models are in fair agreement with the \noverall forecast, and are definitely put us in a dry slot overnight, \nbeginning later this evening. \n \nThe most concerning time for severe weather and flooding potential \nwill be during the afternoon as a low pressure system nears the \narea. There is decent instability and shear through a deep layer. \nLCLs are also expected to be low. While the threat is conditional, \nthere is the potential for a few short lived tornadoes to occur in \nthis environment. The greatest threat area for tornadoes will be \neastern Indiana, portions of northern Kentucky and southwest Ohio. \nAnother potential threat will be damaging winds, however DCAPE\nremains not overly impressive. \n\nThe greatest threat area for flooding will also be the same area as\ntornadoes, aligned with more significant storm structures. Storms\nwill be slow moving, and some few areas may be hit with training\nstorms that will significantly increase the threat of flooding.\n \nAdditional thunderstorm on Sunday will be a continuation from the\nsame slow moving low pressure systems with widespread showers and \nsome thunderstorms. The greater severe threat will be east of the \nScioto River Valley and in the Hocking Hills. Cannot rule out a \nstrong to severe storm. In addition, localized heavy rain and \nflooding will remain possible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nOff and on thunderstorm chances will continue into the work week. The\npath of the upper low remains in a high state of uncertainty, though\na number of models show it crossing the CWA Monday and then\nretrograding back into the region on Tuesday. It doesn't exit until \nsometime Wednesday. There are indications of another system\npotentially affecting the CWA and region on Friday.",
